window.SLIDE_TEXT = window.SLIDE_TEXT || {};
window.SLIDE_TEXT["cad-part-2"] = {"1":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Disease Part II:</li><li>Prevention &amp; Risk Modification</li><li>PHAS 517</li><li>Jessica Stager, MPAS, PA-C</li><li>November 18, 2026</li></ul>",
"2":"<ul><li>Primary Care and CAD</li><li>Primary Prevention:</li><li>Reduce modifiable risk factors through lifestyle modification and early identification of metabolic abnormalities to prevent the development of CAD</li><li>Secondary Prevention:</li><li>Prevent progression of established atherosclerosis, preventing further cardiac damage, and reducing the risk of recurrent cardiovascular events in patients who already have a diagnosis of CAD.</li></ul>",
"3":"<ul><li>Primary Care and CAD</li><li>Cardiovascular Risk Assessment in Primary Care:</li><li>Assess cardiovascular risk factors as part of routine preventive care.</li><li>Evaluate blood pressure, lipid levels, smoking status, diabetes, kidney function, and lifestyle.</li><li>Estimate cardiovascular risk when appropriate to guide preventive treatment decisions.</li><li>Reassess risk as clinical circumstances change.</li></ul>",
"4":"<ul><li>Primary Care and CAD</li><li>Patients ages 20 to 29 years –<ul><li>Identify and address cardiovascular risk factors early.</li><li>Assess lifetime risk and family history when appropriate.</li><li>Consider earlier lipid-lowering treatment in selected young adults, particularly those with LDL-C ≥160 mg/dL or a strong family history of premature ASCVD.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"5":"<ul><li>Primary Care and CAD</li><li>Patients ages 30 to 79 years –<ul><li>Use PREVENT-ASCVD for eligible patients to estimate 10-year risk.</li><li>Consider 30-year risk in eligible adults ages 30–59.</li><li>Use the estimate alongside clinical risk factors to guide prevention.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"6":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD</li><li>2026 Update: PREVENT-ASCVD → preferred risk calculator</li><li>Three models are available:</li><li>PREVENT-ASCVD: Estimates the risk of myocardial infarction and stroke.</li><li>PREVENT-HF: Estimates the risk of heart failure.</li><li>PREVENT-CVD: Estimates total cardiovascular disease risk, including ASCVD and heart failure.</li><li>Time frames</li><li>10-year risk for eligible adults ages 30–79.</li><li>30-year risk for eligible adults ages 30–59.</li><li>For lipid-lowering decisions: Use PREVENT-ASCVD.</li></ul>",
"7":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD vs PCE</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Patient factor</td><td>PCE</td><td>PREVENT</td></tr><tr><td>Age</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Sex</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Total cholesterol</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>HDL cholesterol</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Systolic blood pressure</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Antihypertensive treatment</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Diabetes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Smoking</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Kidney function (eGFR)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Body mass index (BMI)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio</td><td>No</td><td>Optional additional predictor</td></tr><tr><td>Hemoglobin A1c</td><td>No</td><td>Optional additional predictor</td></tr><tr><td>Social deprivation index</td><td>No</td><td>Optional additional predictor</td></tr></table>",
"8":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD vs PCE</li><li>A. Kidney function: eGFR</li><li>The PREVENT base equations incorporate estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR), a measure of kidney function.</li><li>Why is this important?</li><li>Reduced kidney function is associated with increased cardiovascular risk.</li><li>CKD and cardiovascular disease frequently coexist.</li><li>GFR helps capture risk associated with impaired kidney function that the PCE does not directly incorporate.</li></ul>",
"9":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD vs PCE</li><li>B. Metabolic health: BMI and HbA1c</li><li>These measures help capture aspects of metabolic health beyond simply identifying whether a patient has diabetes.</li><li>BMI</li><li>Provides information about body size and obesity-related risk.</li><li>Helps incorporate metabolic health into cardiovascular risk estimation.</li><li>HbA1c</li><li>Available as an optional additional predictor.</li><li>Reflects average blood glucose over approximately the preceding 2–3 months.</li><li>Can provide additional information about glycemic status beyond a simple yes/no diabetes variable.</li></ul>",
"10":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD vs PCE</li><li>C. Kidney damage: Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR)</li><li>UACR assesses urinary albumin excretion relative to urinary creatinine. It helps identify kidney damage, including damage that may occur before substantial reductions in eGFR.</li><li>Albuminuria provides additional information about kidney and cardiovascular health.</li><li>For example, a patient with diabetes and a normal eGFR may still have significant albuminuria. UACR can help identify this additional risk.</li></ul>",
"11":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD vs PCE</li><li>D. PREVENT also offers an optional Social Deprivation Index based on the patient's ZIP code.</li><li>This measure reflects area-level social and economic conditions that may influence health, such as:</li><li>Access to healthcare.</li><li>Economic resources.</li><li>Educational opportunities.</li><li>Housing and other social conditions.</li></ul>",
"12":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD</li><li>Patient Eligibility:</li><li>Use PREVENT-ASCVD for eligible adults ages 30–79 without known CVD or subclinical atherosclerosis who fall within the applicable model's parameters.</li><li>Do not use PREVENT as the primary decision tool for patients with:</li><li>Known cardiovascular disease.</li><li>Severe subclinical cardiovascular disease, such as CAC ≥300.</li><li>End-stage kidney disease.</li><li>A life expectancy of less than one year.</li><li>Certain inherited cardiovascular conditions that independently establish elevated risk.</li></ul>",
"13":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>10-year PREVENT-ASCVD risk</td><td>Risk category</td></tr><tr><td>&lt;3%</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>3% to &lt;5%</td><td>Borderline</td></tr><tr><td>5% to &lt;10%</td><td>Intermediate</td></tr><tr><td>≥10%</td><td>High</td></tr></table>",
"14":"<ul><li>PREVENT-ASCVD</li><li>https://professional.heart.org/en/guidelines-and-statements/prevent-calculator</li></ul>",
"15":"<ul><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk Modification</li><li>Any approach to CV risk modification begins with patient education.</li><li>For those patients who are healthy, emphasis is placed on continuation of healthy habits.</li><li>For those patients with unhealthy habits, the counseling is more extensive, and the provider should set specific attainable goals for change with the patient.</li></ul>",
"16":"<ul><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk Modification: Good Habits</li><li>1. Follow a heart-healthy diet</li><li>Emphasize fruits, vegetables, whole grains, legumes, nuts, fish, and lean protein.</li><li>Replace saturated fats with unsaturated fats (olive oil, nuts, avocados, and fatty fish).</li><li>Choose low-fat or fat-free dairy products.</li><li>2. Limit foods that increase cardiovascular risk</li><li>Limit saturated fats, trans fats, processed meats, and highly processed foods.</li><li>Reduce sodium intake.</li><li>Limit added sugars and sugar-sweetened beverages.</li></ul>",
"17":"<ul><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk Modification: Good Habits</li><li>3. Maintain adequate hydration</li><li>Choose water over sugar-sweetened beverages.</li><li>4. Engage in regular physical activity</li><li>At least 150 minutes/week of moderate-intensity OR 75 minutes/week of vigorous-intensity aerobic activity.</li><li>Include muscle-strengthening activities at least 2 days/week.</li><li>5. Prioritize adequate sleep</li><li>Aim for 7–9 hours of sleep per night.</li></ul>",
"18":"<ul><li>How do U.S. adults spend their day?</li><li>Average time spent across a 24-hour day</li><li>SEDENTARY TIME</li><li>&gt;7 hours</li><li>spent in sedentary activities each day</li><li>MODERATE/VIGOROUS ACTIVITY</li><li>0.2 hours</li><li>≈12 minutes per day</li><li>1 in 3 adults gets less than 7 hours of sleep.</li><li>Hours per day</li><li>Vigorous activity</li><li>Sedentary</li><li>Light activity</li><li>Sleep</li></ul>",
"19":"",
"20":"",
"21":"<ul><li>How Exercise Protects the Heart</li><li>Regular activity benefits cardiovascular health in several ways.</li><li>ANTIATHEROGENIC EFFECTS</li><li>↓</li><li>Less abdominal fat</li><li>↓</li><li>Lower blood pressure</li><li>↓</li><li>Lower triglycerides</li><li>↑</li><li>Higher HDL</li><li>✓</li><li>Better insulin sensitivity</li><li>OTHER CARDIOVASCULAR BENEFITS</li><li>ANTITHROMBOTIC</li><li>ENDOTHELIAL</li><li>FUNCTION</li><li>AUTONOMIC</li><li>FUNCTION</li><li>ANTIISCHEMIC</li><li>ANTIARRHYTHMIC</li><li>Exercise lowers cardiovascular risk through multiple pathways.</li><li>Reference: UpToDate; Fletcher GF, Balady GJ, Amsterdam EA, et al. Circulation. 2001;104:1694.</li></ul>",
"22":"<ul><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk ModificationLipid Management</li><li>One of the most important diseases to control, if we want to prevent CAD, is dyslipidemia.</li><li>We primarily do this with statins!</li><li>High Intensity Statins<ul><li>Atorvastatin (Lipitor) 40 to 80 mg once daily</li><li>Rosuvastatin (Crestor) 20 to 40 mg once daily</li></ul></li><li>Moderate Intensity Statins<ul><li>Atorvastatin (Lipitor) 10-20mg; Rosuvastatin(Crestor) 5-10 mg; Lovastatin (Mevacor) 40 mg; Pravastatin (Pravachol) 40 mg; Simvastatin (Zocor) 40mg.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"23":"<ul><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk ModificationLipid Management Secondary Prevention</li><li>All patients with clinical atherosclerotic disease should be on a high intensity statin, regardless of their lipid profile.<ul><li>Coronary artery disease: ACS, prior MI, stable or unstable angina, PCI/stent, or CABG</li><li>Cerebrovascular disease: Atherosclerotic ischemic stroke or TIA</li><li>Peripheral artery disease: Atherosclerotic PAD</li><li>Aortic disease: Atherosclerotic aortic aneurysm</li></ul></li></ul>",
"24":"<ul><li>For primary prevention, the 2026 dyslipidemia guideline recommends lipid-lowering therapy in specified patient groups regardless of the calculated PREVENT-ASCVD risk.</li><li>Severe hypercholesterolemia: LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL.</li><li>Diabetes: Adults ages 40–75 with diabetes.</li><li>Chronic kidney disease: Adults ages 40–75 with stage 3 or 4 CKD.</li><li>HIV: Adults ages 40–75 living with HIV.</li><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk ModificationLipid Management Primary Prevention</li></ul>",
"25":"<ul><li>Start With the Lipid Panel</li><li>Review the individual components—not just total cholesterol.</li><li>A standard lipid panel includes LDL-C, HDL-C, total cholesterol, and triglycerides.</li><li>LIPID</li><li>DESIRABLE</li><li>BORDERLINE</li><li>HIGH / VERY HIGH</li><li>LDL-C</li><li>&lt;100 mg/dL</li><li>130–159 mg/dL</li><li>160–189: high</li><li>Total-C</li><li>&lt;200 mg/dL</li><li>200–239 mg/dL</li><li>≥240 mg/dL: high</li><li>HDL-C</li><li>≥60 mg/dL</li><li>—</li><li>&lt;40 mg/dL (men)</li><li>Triglycerides</li><li>&lt;150 mg/dL</li><li>150–199 mg/dL</li><li>200–499: high</li><li>Important: These are conventional lipid classifications—not individualized LDL treatment goals. LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL is a distinct severe-hypercholesterolemia category.</li><li>Values in mg/dL. Interpret results in the context of overall ASCVD risk and current guidelines.</li></ul>",
"26":"<ul><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk Modification: Lipid Management Primary Prevention</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Patient group</td><td>Recommended approach</td></tr><tr><td>LDL-C ≥190 mg/dL</td><td>Initiate lipid-lowering therapy without relying on calculated risk; evaluate for familial hypercholesterolemia when appropriate.</td></tr><tr><td>Adults ages 40–75 with diabetes, stage 3–4 CKD, or HIV</td><td>Lipid-lowering therapy is recommended regardless of calculated PREVENT-ASCVD risk.</td></tr><tr><td>Eligible adults ages 30–79 with LDL-C 70–189 mg/dL and no established ASCVD or subclinical atherosclerosis</td><td>Use PREVENT-ASCVD to guide primary-prevention decisions.</td></tr></table>",
"27":"<ul><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk Modification: Lipid Management Primary Prevention</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>PREVENT-ASCVD risk</td><td>General approach to lipid-lowering therapy</td></tr><tr><td>Low: &lt;3%</td><td>Lifestyle management is central. Consider lipid-lowering therapy in selected patients with LDL-C 160–189 mg/dL or a 30-year ASCVD risk ≥10% in eligible adults ages 30–59.</td></tr><tr><td>Borderline: 3% to &lt;5%</td><td>Lipid-lowering therapy may be considered after a clinician-patient discussion.</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate: 5% to &lt;10%</td><td>Lipid-lowering therapy should be considered after a clinician-patient discussion.</td></tr><tr><td>High: ≥10%</td><td>Lipid-lowering therapy is recommended</td></tr></table>",
"28":"<ul><li>Primary Care Approach to CV Risk Modification: Lipid Management Primary Prevention</li><li>1. Calculate</li><li>Estimate 10-year PREVENT-ASCVD risk.</li><li>Consider 30-year risk in eligible adults ages 30–59.</li><li>2. Personalize</li><li>Family history of premature ASCVD.</li><li>Persistent LDL-C elevation.</li><li>Elevated Lp(a).</li><li>Chronic inflammatory conditions.</li><li>Other relevant clinical and lipid-related risk factors.</li><li>3. Reclassify</li><li>Consider CAC scoring when the treatment decision remains uncertain in an appropriate patient.</li></ul>",
"29":"<ul><li>Personalizing ASCVD Risk Beyond PREVENT-ASCVD</li><li>1. Family history and demographics</li><li>Premature ASCVD in a parent or sibling</li><li>Higher-risk ancestry (South Asian, Filipino)</li><li>2. Lipid-related risk enhancers</li><li>Elevated Lp(a): ≥125 nmol/L or ≥50 mg/dL</li><li>Persistent LDL-C 160–189 mg/dL</li><li>Elevated ApoB ≥120 mg/dL or non-HDL-C 190–219 mg/dL</li><li>Persistent elevated triglycerides</li></ul>",
"30":"<ul><li>Personalizing ASCVD Risk Beyond PREVENT-ASCVD</li><li>3. Inflammatory and metabolic conditions</li><li>Chronic inflammatory disease (RA, SLE, advanced psoriasis)</li><li>hsCRP ≥2 mg/L on more than one occasion</li><li>4. Reproductive risk factors</li><li>Premature menopause (before age 40)</li><li>Preeclampsia</li><li>Gestational diabetes or hypertension</li><li>Preterm delivery</li></ul>",
"31":"<ul><li>Lipoprotein A - Lp(a)</li><li>LDL-like particle with an additional apolipoprotein(a) attached to apoB-100.</li><li>Levels are largely genetically determined.</li><li>Measure at least once in all adults.</li><li>Elevated: ≥125 nmol/L (50 mg/dL) — approximately 1.4-fold increased ASCVD risk.</li><li>Very high: ≥250 nmol/L (100 mg/dL) — approximately 2-fold increased estimated ASCVD risk.</li><li>Elevated Lp(a) warrants more intensive management of modifiable risk factors and LDL-C lowering.</li></ul>",
"32":"<ul><li>Apolipoprotein B (ApoB)</li><li>ApoB measures the number of circulating atherogenic lipoprotein particles.</li><li>An elevated ApoB suggests increased atherogenic particle burden.</li><li>ApoB can reveal risk that LDL-C alone may underestimate, particularly in patients with elevated triglycerides, diabetes, or CKM syndrome.</li><li>ApoB ≥120 mg/dL = ASCVD risk-enhancing factor (2026 guideline).</li></ul>",
"33":"<ul><li>ApoB vs. Lp(a)</li><li>Apolipoprotein B (ApoB)</li><li>Measures the number of atherogenic lipoprotein particles.</li><li>Each atherogenic particle carries one ApoB molecule.</li><li>Includes LDL, VLDL, IDL, and Lp(a).</li><li>Think: Particle number</li><li>Lipoprotein(a) [Lp(a)]</li><li>A specific, inherited type of LDL-like particle.</li><li>Has an additional protein called apolipoprotein(a) attached to ApoB.</li><li>Elevated levels increase ASCVD risk.</li><li>Think: Inherited risk particle</li></ul>",
"34":"<ul><li>High-Sensitivity C-Reactive Protein (hs-CRP)</li><li>CRP is a marker of systemic inflammation.</li><li>Elevated hs-CRP is associated with increased ASCVD risk.</li><li>In adults without ASCVD who have borderline PREVENT-ASCVD risk (3% to less than 5%), if hs-CRP is:<ul><li>≥2 mg/L on two successive occasions</li><li>With no identifiable underlying cause</li></ul></li><li>Then high-intensity statin therapy can be useful to reduce ASCVD events</li></ul>",
"35":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Calcium (CAC) Scoring</li><li>Consider CAC scoring in selected adults at borderline or intermediate PREVENT-ASCVD risk when uncertainty about lipid-lowering therapy remains.</li><li>CAC provides evidence of calcified coronary atherosclerosis and can help refine risk.<ul><li>CAC = 0: May support deferring therapy in selected patients, provided important exceptions—such as diabetes, smoking, and a strong family history of premature ASCVD—are absent.</li><li>CAC 1–99: Indicates coronary atherosclerosis; the treatment decision should incorporate the patient's overall risk and CAC percentile.</li><li>CAC ≥100 or ≥75th percentile: Supports lipid-lowering therapy.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"36":"<ul><li>CV Risk Modification</li><li>Okay, we have addressed the healthy habits that a person needs to modify their risk of CAD.</li><li>We have addressed their lipids to prevent CAD.</li><li>What is next??<ul><li>Diabetes</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Sleep apnea</li><li>Alcohol/ Smoking</li></ul></li></ul>",
"37":"<ul><li>Diabetics and CV Risk Modification</li><li>Glycemic control</li><li>Optimize glucose control using individualized A1c targets.</li><li>A1c reflects average blood glucose over approximately 2–3 months.</li><li>Diagnostic criteria:</li><li>Additional diabetes criteria:</li><li>Fasting plasma glucose ≥126 mg/dL</li><li>2-hour OGTT glucose ≥200 mg/dL</li><li>Random glucose ≥200 mg/dL with classic symptoms</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Category</td><td>A1c</td></tr><tr><td>Prediabetes</td><td>5.7–6.4%</td></tr><tr><td>Diabetes</td><td>≥6.5%</td></tr></table>",
"38":"<ul><li>Diabetics and CV Risk Modification</li><li>Adult DM treatment goals:<ul><li>Many nonpregnant adults: An A1c goal below 7% is appropriate.</li><li>Selected patients: A goal below 6.5% may be appropriate when achievable without significant hypoglycemia or other treatment burden.</li><li>Patients with substantial comorbidity, frailty, limited life expectancy, or high treatment-related risk: A less stringent goal, such as below 8%, may be appropriate.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"39":"<ul><li>Diabetics and CV Risk Modification</li><li>In patients with type 2 diabetes and established ASCVD, heart failure, or CKD, use a GLP-1 RA and/or SGLT2 inhibitor with demonstrated cardiovascular or kidney benefit, as appropriate.</li><li>These medications may be indicated regardless of baseline A1c or metformin use.</li><li>Drug selection depends on cardiovascular and kidney conditions, glycemic needs, weight goals, contraindications, tolerability, and patient preferences.</li></ul>",
"40":"<ul><li>Diabetics and CV Risk Modification</li><li>Certain features of diabetes increase the risk of CVD in the person with diabetes:<ul><li>Long duration of disease (≥10 years for T2DM, ≥20 years for T1DM), why the difference?</li><li>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio (UACR) ≥30 mg/g</li><li>eGFR &lt;60 mL/min/1.73 m2</li><li>Diabetic retinopathy</li><li>Diabetic neuropathy- of any type</li><li>ABI &lt; 0.9</li></ul></li></ul>",
"41":"<ul><li>Other Aspects of CV Risk Modification</li><li>Diagnose and treat hypertension to goal</li><li>Confirm hypertension using standardized office BP measurements and home or ambulatory monitoring when appropriate.</li><li>General treatment goal: BP below 130/80 mmHg for most adults.</li><li>Consider more intensive SBP lowering (below 120 mmHg) in appropriate patients at increased cardiovascular risk.</li><li>Individualize treatment based on comorbidities, orthostatic symptoms, frailty, and medication tolerance.</li><li>Select antihypertensive therapy based on the patient's clinical profile and compelling indications.</li></ul>",
"42":"<ul><li>Other Aspects of CV Risk Modification</li><li>Recognize and treat obstructive sleep apnea (OSA)</li><li>Recurrent upper-airway obstruction during sleep causes intermittent hypoxemia (repeated drops in oxygen saturation).</li><li>Brief arousals restore airway patency and breathing but fragment normal sleep.</li><li>Each episode triggers sympathetic activation, increasing heart rate and blood pressure.</li><li>Repeated episodes contribute to inflammation, endothelial dysfunction, and cardiovascular stress.</li></ul>",
"43":"<ul><li>Other Aspects of CV Risk Modification</li><li>Persons with untreated sleep apnea develop RV hypertrophy and pulmonary hypertension, as the body attempts to desperately increase oxygenation to the tissues by circulating the blood through the lungs more quickly.</li><li>Eventually, these patients will develop right sided heart failure (cor pulmonale).</li><li>Treatment by wearing their CPAP or BiPAP at night every night is essential.</li></ul>",
"44":"<ul><li>Other Aspects of CV Risk Modification</li><li>Screen for unhealthy alcohol use</li><li>Cardiovascular risk increases with greater alcohol consumption; heavy and binge drinking are particularly harmful.</li><li>Recognize excessive drinking</li><li>Binge: ≥4 drinks for women or ≥5 for men on one occasion</li><li>Heavy: ≥8 drinks/week for women or ≥15 drinks/week for men</li></ul>",
"45":"<ul><li>Other Aspects of CV Risk Modification</li><li>One drink =</li><li>12 oz of 4-5% ABV beer</li><li>5 oz of wine</li><li>1.5 oz hard liquor</li><li>Most patients go above this easily</li></ul>",
"46":"<ul><li>Other Aspects of CV Risk Modification</li><li>Counsel patients</li><li>For patients who drink, encourage reduction or abstinence.</li><li>For BP management, the 2025 guideline recommends abstinence as the optimal goal; if drinking continues, limit intake to ≤1 drink/day for women and ≤2 for men.</li></ul>",
"47":"<ul><li>Other Aspects of CV Risk Modification</li><li>Smoking cessation is addressed at every visit<ul><li>Ask the patient and document the amount they are smoking.  One pack = 20 cigarettes</li><li>Ask if they have tried to quit or cut down- if so, how much.  If not, why not?</li><li>Have they tried an adjunct to quit smoking?  Adjunct use doubles success rate.</li><li>Chewing tobacco use or vaping?</li></ul></li></ul>",
"48":"<ul><li>Smoking Cessation</li><li>Three-Step Model:</li><li>Ask</li><li>Advise</li><li>Connect</li><li>Five A’s</li><li>Ask</li><li>Advise</li><li>Assess</li><li>Assist</li><li>Arrange follow-up</li><li>Ask all patient about tobacco use</li><li>Advise quitting</li></ul>",
"49":"<ul><li>Smoking Cessation Adjuncts</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Form</td><td>Dosage</td><td>Precautions</td></tr><tr><td>Nicotine Patch</td><td>21 mg if &gt; ½ ppd 14 mg if &lt; ½ ppd</td><td>Local irritation, remove for sleep</td></tr><tr><td>Nicotine Gum/ Lozenge</td><td>4 mg if first tobacco is ≤ 30 min after waking 2mg if &gt; 30 min.  1 piece every 1-2 hours. Max 20 lozenges or 24 pieces of gum/day</td><td>Hiccups, GERD, Avoid 15 min before and after eating</td></tr><tr><td>Nasal or oral inhaler</td><td>1-2 nasal sprays per hour or puff on oral cartridge for 20 min/ hour</td><td>Local irritation, avoid with nasal/ airway disease</td></tr></table>",
"50":"<ul><li>Smoking Cessation Adjuncts</li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Form</td><td>Dosage</td><td>Precautions</td></tr><tr><td>Buproprion (Zyban or Wellbutrin)</td><td>150 mg/day x 3 days then 150 mg twice daily</td><td>Avoid with h/o or risk of seizures, eating disorders, avoid with MAOIs</td></tr><tr><td>Varenicline (Chantix)</td><td>0.5 mg daily x 3 days, then 0.5 mg twice daily x 4 days, then 1 mg twice daily</td><td>Nausea common, can cause vivid dreams, must be dose adjusted for CKD</td></tr></table>",
"51":"<ul><li>Take Home Messages for CAD Prevention</li><li>Lifestyle changes, lifestyle changes, lifestyle changes!  They rely on a motivated patient and are the hardest to accomplish but lifestyle changes make the greatest impact on ASCVD risk.  Keep chipping away and working with your patient to find goals that they can accomplish.</li><li>The importance of controlling these gateway diseases to goal- hypertension, dyslipidemia and diabetes- in preventing CAD cannot be overstated.</li></ul>",
"52":"<ul><li>Aspirin for Primary Prevention: Use Selectively</li><li>Routine low-dose aspirin (81 mg/day) is not recommended for most adults without established ASCVD.</li><li>Aspirin may be considered in select adults ages 40–70 with elevated cardiovascular risk and low bleeding risk.</li><li>Do not routinely initiate aspirin in adults older than 70 or patients with increased bleeding risk.</li><li>Key takeaway</li><li>The decision requires individualized assessment of cardiovascular benefit versus bleeding risk.</li></ul>",
"53":"<ul><li>Thomas is a 52-year-old Caucasian man with history of diet-controlled dyslipidemia and tobacco abuse.</li><li>He presents to your office today for a wellness check after switching PCP offices for insurance reasons.</li><li>CVD Prevention Example</li></ul>",
"54":"<ul><li>CVD Prevention Example</li><li>In the office today, Thomas admits that he smokes 1ppd and has done so since age 17.  He feels that he gets “exercise” at his active job as the meat department manager at a local grocery store.</li><li>His BMI is 28 (normal 18-24.9) with an abdominal circumference of 110 cm (43.3 inches) and he worries that if he cuts down on smoking, he will gain more weight.</li></ul>",
"55":"<ul><li>CVD Prevention Example</li><li>You acknowledge that weight gain with smoking cessation is a concern, but none the less, advise Thomas that the #1 intervention he can do now for his health is to quit smoking.</li><li>After a discussion, he refuses an adjunct at this time.</li><li>You ask him to “think hard” about the need for each cigarette and try to cut down.</li></ul>",
"56":"<ul><li>CVD Prevention Example</li><li>You encourage Thomas to get out and do some brisk walking most days of the week for about 20 minutes, which should help with any weight gain he has.</li><li>His exam is normal with a BP of 117/68.</li><li>You order a CBC, chemistry panel, fasting lipid panel and A1c, asking Thomas to follow-up in the office in 3 months to discuss the results.</li></ul>",
"57":"<ul><li>CVD Prevention Example</li><li>Thomas follows-up as directed and “for the most part” has followed your advice.  He is down to ½ ppd. He has also maintained his weight.</li><li>Thomas’s blood work returns, and the good news is that his A1c is 4.8%.  Unfortunately, his lipid panel shows problems; TC: 210, LDLc: 108, HDL: 32, trig: 398 and his GFR is 55.  Today his BP is 120/60 and his physical exam is normal.</li></ul>",
"58":"<ul><li>CVD Prevention Example</li><li>Thomas is seen annually and manages to quit smoking for about 2 years.  When he is 55 his wife is diagnosed with breast cancer, and he begins to smoke again.</li><li>His wife is in treatment and Thomas is seeing a therapist on-line to deal with depression.  Today, his A1c is 7.7% and his lipid panel is TC: 160, LDLc: 88, HDL: 38, trig: 258.  BP= 120/70.</li><li>What do you do?</li></ul>",
"59":"<ul><li>CVD Prevention Example</li><li>Despite your best efforts, Thomas continues to smoke and is diagnosed with hypertension within a year of his diabetes diagnosis.  You begin an ARB to bring his BP to &lt; 130/80.</li><li>You continue to aggressively try to manage his risk factors, but at age 66, 14 years after you took over his care, Thomas suffers an NSTEMI and undergoes a 3-vessel CABG (coronary artery bypass grafting) or bypass surgery.  We’ll learn soon how his management will change.</li></ul>",
"60":"<ul><li>Take-Home MessagesPrimary Prevention of CAD</li><li>Start early. Atherosclerosis develops over time, and prevention should begin before short-term risk becomes high.</li><li>Calculate appropriately. Use PREVENT-ASCVD to estimate 10-year risk in eligible adults and consider 30-year risk in eligible younger adults.</li><li>Recognize independent treatment indications. Severe hypercholesterolemia, diabetes, and stage 3–4 CKD can warrant lipid-lowering therapy without relying on calculated risk.</li><li>Personalize the estimate. Consider family history, Lp(a), other risk factors, and patient preferences.</li></ul>",
"61":"<ul><li>Take-Home MessagesPrimary Prevention of CAD</li><li>Reclassify when appropriate. CAC scoring can help resolve uncertainty about treatment.</li><li>Treat to goals. Follow both the recommended percentage LDL-C reduction and applicable LDL-C/non-HDL-C goals.</li><li>Address all major risk factors. Smoking cessation, physical activity, nutrition, blood pressure, and diabetes management remain essential.</li></ul>"};
